diagnosis_text:
Every diagnostic layer was inspected and found completely healthy. Phase 1 reachability showed host_1_1_1_1 reaching 10.200.0.3 (web_server_0) and 10.200.0.5 (web_server_2) with 0% packet loss. The Phase A infra_sweep confirmed host_1_1_1_1 has a valid IP address, a correct default gateway, and a clean ARP entry for its upstream router (10.1.1.1). The l2_snapshot found no duplicate MAC addresses and no down or missing interfaces on any of the 15 devices. Phase B ospf_snapshot showed all six FRR routers — including switch_dist_1_1 (the L3 gateway for the 10.1.1.0/24 subnet) — with fully converged OSPF adjacencies and ten OSPF routes each, meaning the path from host_1_1_1_1 through router_core_1 to switch_server_access and on to 10.200.0.0/24 is intact. The tc_snapshot found only default fq_codel qdiscs with no bandwidth throttling or corruption. Phase D service_snapshot confirmed host_1_1_1_1 successfully resolves web0–3.local via DNS (10.200.0.2) and receives HTTP 200 responses from all four web servers in 4–6 ms. Pressure_sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion on any device. The user's reported fault — inability to reach 10.200.0.x — is unconfirmed by any live evidence; no mechanism, historical trace, or active fault condition exists. Root cause: no anomaly is present; the network is fully operational at the time of this investigation.
